# Copy to .env and fill in the five REQUIRED values below. Compose refuses to start until
# every one of them is set — there are no default credentials and no default domain
# anywhere. `make up` generates the three passwords for a LOCAL run; a public deploy needs
# the two lines under "Your domain" as well.
#
# The Django SECRET_KEY is deliberately NOT set here. The container generates it and
# persists it at first boot (docs/security/threat-model.md, TM-8).
#
# --- REQUIRED: three database roles (ADR-004 role split, threat model TS-PG-1) ---------
#   POSTGRES_PASSWORD          the bootstrap superuser; initdb, healthchecks, backups only
#   POSTGRES_MIGRATOR_PASSWORD backyard_migrator; owns the schema, runs migrations
#   POSTGRES_APP_PASSWORD      backyard_app; what the application runs as (no DDL)

POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
POSTGRES_MIGRATOR_PASSWORD=
POSTGRES_APP_PASSWORD=

# --- REQUIRED for a public deploy: your domain ------------------------------------------
# Only the production overlay (`-f docker-compose.prod.yml`) reads these, and it requires
# both: it will refuse to start rather than come up on the wrong hostname or without a
# certificate. A purely local run on http://localhost:8000 does not need them.
#
# BACKYARD_DOMAIN is the single source of the public hostname. The overlay DERIVES
# DJANGO_ALLOWED_HOSTS and BACKYARD_BASE_URL from it, and the HTTPS security posture
# (Secure cookies, HSTS, SSL redirect) keys off that base URL's scheme — so you cannot set
# the domain, forget the base URL, and get a silently-insecure-but-working instance.
#
# ACME_EMAIL is your real e-mail, given to the certificate authority. It is required rather
# than optional (TS-CA-5): it is where certificate-expiry warnings go, and it keeps the
# ZeroSSL failover issuer available if Let's Encrypt is unreachable. An empty value fails
# Caddy's config parse, which fails closed — no plaintext fallback.

BACKYARD_DOMAIN=
ACME_EMAIL=

# Optional overrides (defaults shown):
# DJANGO_DEBUG=0
# DJANGO_ALLOWED_HOSTS=localhost,127.0.0.1
# BACKYARD_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8000
#
# The BACKYARD_BASE_URL default is correct only while this instance serves nobody but the
# machine it runs on. Every invite link, elder link and digest link is built from it, so if
# you set DJANGO_ALLOWED_HOSTS to a real domain by hand and leave this at localhost, every
# link you hand out is dead on arrival for the person who receives it. The app refuses to
# start in that combination rather than mint links nobody can open. The production overlay
# derives both from BACKYARD_DOMAIN, so it is already right there.
# POSTGRES_DB=backyard
# POSTGRES_USER=backyard

# --- Your time zone ------------------------------------------------------
# The clock your family reads. SET THIS: the default is UTC, which for most families is
# several hours wrong, and the product states the time with no hedge — a post written at
# 4:28 in the morning read "9:28 a.m." on a real instance before this setting existed.
# It is what the feed, a thread, an invite's last day and the Family email are written in.
#
# An IANA name (America/Chicago, Europe/London, Australia/Sydney). A typo refuses to boot
# and names this variable, rather than booting and printing wrong times on every screen:
#   python3 -c 'import zoneinfo; print(sorted(zoneinfo.available_timezones()))'
#
# A signed-in relative in a DIFFERENT zone needs nothing: each page carries the instant in
# its markup and their own browser re-renders it into their zone. This setting is what an
# e-mail uses, because an e-mail is written here and read with no browser to correct it.
# Safe to change later — every timestamp is stored in UTC, so this only changes the display.
# BACKYARD_TIME_ZONE=UTC

# --- Email ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Out of the box the console backend prints mail to the container log, so an
# unconfigured instance works and sends nothing.
#
# The NAME beside your address in a relative's inbox. Mail from an unnamed sender arrives
# as "digests@mail.example" — or just "digests" in the clients that shorten it — which is
# how a family's own photographs come to look like something a spam filter should eat.
# Applied to EVERY message this instance sends, including the address confirmation, which
# is often the first thing it ever sends anybody. Your family's name works well here.
# BACKYARD_MAIL_FROM_NAME=Backyard
#
# OPTION A — your own SMTP server (no SaaS account, no vendor).
# Set the backend AND a host: the boot guard refuses to start with an SMTP
# backend and an empty EMAIL_HOST rather than silently dropping every message.
# EMAIL_BACKEND=django.core.mail.backends.smtp.EmailBackend
# EMAIL_HOST=smtp.example.com
# EMAIL_PORT=587
# EMAIL_HOST_USER=backyard@example.com
# EMAIL_HOST_PASSWORD=
# EMAIL_USE_TLS=1          # 1 for STARTTLS on 587; use EMAIL_USE_SSL=1 on 465 instead
# EMAIL_USE_SSL=0
# DEFAULT_FROM_EMAIL=backyard@example.com
#
# Honest limitation: this covers OUTBOUND only. Reply-by-email (S-502) needs an
# inbound provider that posts a webhook, and today that path is Resend-only.
# With SMTP alone the digest still sends; replies must be made on the web.
#
# OPTION B — Resend (hosted; the only path that also does inbound replies).
# EMAIL_BACKEND=anymail.backends.resend.EmailBackend
# RESEND_API_KEY=
# RESEND_INBOUND_SECRET=   # required whenever the Resend backend is active (TS-PP-8)
# DEFAULT_FROM_EMAIL=backyard@mail.example.com

# --- Backups (S-802) -----------------------------------------------------
# Backups are ENCRYPTED BY DEFAULT. Set a strong passphrase here (or point the
# command at --passphrase-file); backup_instance refuses to write a plaintext
# archive unless you also pass --no-encrypt explicitly.
# This one is load-bearing rather than optional: the instance takes a backup of
# itself every night at 03:30 UTC, and with no passphrase set that nightly run writes
# NO ARCHIVE AT ALL (it will not fall back to plaintext) and reports the failure
# in the weekly health email and at /healthz.
# There is NO key escrow: lose this and the archive is unrecoverable. Write it
# on the recovery sheet described in docs/runbooks/backup-restore.md.
# BACKYARD_BACKUP_PASSPHRASE=
#
# The tighter option: a 0600 keyfile mounted read-only into the containers, so the
# passphrase is absent from `docker inspect` and from the process environment. Set this to
# the path INSIDE the container (never under /data — a key beside the ciphertext buys
# nothing). Compose passes this variable to BOTH web and worker, so mount the key into
# both: the worker takes the nightly archive, and web's entrypoint dumps the whole database
# before every migration. Either container without it falls back to the passphrase above,
# and with neither set the nightly run refuses and the pre-flight dump is plaintext.
# BACKYARD_BACKUP_PASSPHRASE_FILE=/run/secrets/backyard.key
